runs/council/cursors.json
# TRACKING POLARITY -- giga plan Phase 4 (docs/inventory/2026-08-21/
# GIGA_PLAN_2026-08-22.md:112), the half labelled D15. Generated evidence is
# untracked; gate-exit evidence is tracked. The rule that decides which is
# which: can this file be regenerated from the repository, or is it the only
# copy of a measurement someone made?
#
# `runs/spine/` used to exclude the DIRECTORY, and git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded -- so the two gate-exit receipts written
# into it were invisible no matter what a later negation said. Excluding the
# CONTENTS instead keeps the sqlite spine ignored and lets the receipts through.
runs/spine/*
!runs/spine/gate_discrimination.json
!runs/spine/operability_drill.json
runs/canary/
.serena/
runs/ikarus/
runs/progress/
tests/_looptmp/

# Playwright MCP scratch dir -- tool cache, never source.
.playwright-mcp/

# Watchdog runtime -- giga plan Phase 3 (docs/inventory/2026-08-21/
# GIGA_PLAN_2026-08-22.md, "Watchdog hygiene"). The PowerShell watchdog writes
# attempt logs, sweep logs and state under .claude/watchdog/. Those are traces
# of a loop that already ran -- regenerable, and worthless as history -- while
# the prompt files that DRIVE it are source and stay tracked. Before this block
# only `*.log` caught anything here, so a state or attempt file with any other
# extension was landing in `git status` as a candidate for the next commit.
#
# CONTENTS, not the directory, for exactly the reason the runs/spine block
# above states in full: git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, so `.claude/watchdog/` would silently turn the negation into a
# no-op the moment the tracked prompt is added again on a fresh clone.
.claude/watchdog/*
!.claude/watchdog/docs-sweep-prompt.md

